        ":question/id": "9f144a62-dd63-5191-a104-7945db3746f9",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x) = e^{-4x}$, find $\\frac{df}{dx}$.",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
