        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x) = e^{4x + 7}$, find $\\frac{df}{dx}$.",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=4x+7.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =4{e}^{4x+7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
