        ":question/id": "3b4c3b4f-ee6c-5156-ab65-2225d5039897",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)={x}^{5}-{e}^{4-5x}.$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y={e}^{4-5x}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=4-5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{4-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}-{e}^{4-5x}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}-(-5{e}^{4-5x}) \\\\  & =5({x}^{4}+{e}^{4-5x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
