        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{u}+6,\\,u=-4x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-4x-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=- \\frac{7}{4}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=- \\frac{7}{4}} & =-12{e}^{-4x-6}{|}_{x=- \\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ & =-12{e}^{(-4)(- \\frac{7}{4})-6} \\\\ & =- 12 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=x_1=- \\frac{7}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_1 & =(3{e}^{-4x-6}+6){|}_{x=- \\frac{7}{4}} \\\\ & =3{e}^{(-4)(- \\frac{7}{4})-6}+6 \\\\ & =6 + 3 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6 + 3 e) & =(- 12 e)(x-(- \\frac{7}{4})) \\\\ y-(6 + 3 e) & =- 12 e x-(21 e) \\\\ y & =- 12 e x - 18 e + 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
