      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=5{e}^{6x-4}+1$ at the point where $x=\\frac{5}{6}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{u}+1,\\,u=6x-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=5{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =5{e}^{u}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =30{e}^{u} \\\\  & =30{e}^{6x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{5}{6}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\frac{5}{6}} & =30{e}^{6x-4}{|}_{x=\\frac{5}{6}} \\\\ & =30{e}^{(6)(\\frac{5}{6})-4} \\\\ & =30 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=x_1=\\frac{5}{6}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y_1 & =(5{e}^{6x-4}+1){|}_{x=\\frac{5}{6}} \\\\ & =5{e}^{(6)(\\frac{5}{6})-4}+1 \\\\ & =1 + 5 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1 + 5 e) & =(30 e)(x-(\\frac{5}{6})) \\\\ y-(1 + 5 e) & =30 e x-(25 e) \\\\ y & =30 e x - 20 e + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
